Due in part to quantum mechanics, it is often impossible to know the labels of individual examples from real data. However, simulations (via Monte Carlo methods) play a key role in the development and execution of searches for new particles and it is usually possible to have per-instance labels by examining the Monte Carlo truth record. In addition to purely unsupervised and supervised techniques, there is a spectrum of methods with varying levels of supervision, some of which use a mix of information from data and from simulation. Semi-supervised methods55 5 Such a categorisation is not unique, see e.g. [Zhou 2017](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2112.03769#bib.bib48) for an alternative way of defining weak supervision. We follow the established usage in applications of machine learning for particle physics. have labels available for some, but not all data. Weakly-supervised methods have labels for all data, but each label is noisy. These approaches were first studied in HEP in Refs. [Dery et al. 2017](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2112.03769#bib.bib49); [Metodiev et al. 2017](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2112.03769#bib.bib50); [Cohen et al. 2018](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2112.03769#bib.bib51); [Komiske et al. 2018](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2112.03769#bib.bib52). For example, if a set of events is known to be composed of two classes and the class proportions are p0p\_{0} and p1=1−p0p\_{1}=1-p\_{0}, then randomly assigning the label 00 with probability p0p\_{0} would constitute noisy labels. This is the assumption that underlies Learning from Label Proportions [Dery et al. 2017](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2112.03769#bib.bib49). Learning may still be possible even if the proportions are not known as is the case in Classification Without Labels [Metodiev et al. 2017](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2112.03769#bib.bib50).

